# --- your data -----------------------------------------------------------
#
# NOTE: vault/ is deliberately NOT ignored. The jobs commit it on every run —
# that git history IS the audit trail, and it is what lets you ask "what
# changed last night?" and get a real answer. Ignoring it would break the
# system's core guarantee, not just its convenience.
#
# The consequence: once you start using this, YOUR CLONE CONTAINS YOUR PRIVATE
# NOTES. Two safe ways to live with that:
#
#   1. Keep your clone private. Never push it to a public fork.
#   2. Better: point `paths.vault` in config.yaml at a directory outside this
#      repo, with its own private git repo. Absolute paths are honoured, so
#      the code and your notes then never share a history at all.
#
# The empty vault/ skeleton with .gitkeep files is what ships publicly.

# Raw exports waiting to be ingested, and the extracted reference corpus.
to_ingest/
wisdom_md/

# Job logs contain excerpts of your own material.
logs/
*.log

# Your real config: local paths, your tuning.
config.yaml

# Local MCP wiring, generated from the .example files.
.mcp.json

# qmd's local index and generated config. The directory is ignored by its
# contents rather than by name, so the negation below can still take effect —
# `.qmd/` alone would stop git descending into it and `setup.sh` would find no
# template to copy in a fresh clone.
.qmd/*
!.qmd/index.yml.example

# --- derived -------------------------------------------------------------
# Regenerated on every job commit and carries a generation timestamp, so
# committing it would put a diff in every single run whether or not the vault
# actually changed. Rebuild any time with ./bin/dashboard.sh
vault/dashboard.html

# --- editor / runtime ----------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
.DS_Store
.claude/settings.local.json
# Generated per-run; carries loop titles once the vault is in use.
vault/loops/_catalog.md
